# CKS Exam Drill: AppArmor Kernel Hardening

AppArmor is a Linux Security Module (LSM) that confines programs to a limited set of resources (files, capabilities, network). In the CKS exam, you are frequently asked to load an AppArmor profile on cluster nodes and apply it to a Pod.

---

## 1. Key Linux AppArmor Commands

Check status of loaded profiles and enforcement modes:

```bash
sudo aa-status
```

Parse and reload an AppArmor profile into the Linux kernel:

```bash
sudo apparmor_parser -r /etc/apparmor.d/<profile-file>
```

Change enforcement modes:

```bash
sudo aa-enforce /etc/apparmor.d/<profile-file>   # Strict blocking
sudo aa-complain /etc/apparmor.d/<profile-file>  # Log only, do not block
sudo aa-disable /etc/apparmor.d/<profile-file>   # Disable profile
```

---

## 2. Applying AppArmor to Kubernetes Pods

### Native Syntax (Kubernetes 1.30+, GA in 1.34)

In Kubernetes 1.30 and later, AppArmor is configured directly via `securityContext.appArmorProfile`:

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: secured-pod
spec:
  containers:
    - name: app
      image: busybox:1.36.1
      command: ["sh", "-c", "echo 'test' > /tmp/out.txt"]
      securityContext:
        appArmorProfile:
          type: Localhost
          localhostProfile: k8s-deny-write
```

Supported `type` values:

- `RuntimeDefault`: Use container runtime default AppArmor profile.
- `Localhost`: Use profile loaded on the node specified by `localhostProfile`.
- `Unconfined`: Disable AppArmor confinement for the container.

### Legacy Syntax (Kubernetes < 1.30)

> [!NOTE]
> Deprecated in 1.30 and removed in 1.34. Shown for historical exam context:

```yaml
metadata:
  annotations:
    container.apparmor.security.beta.kubernetes.io/<container-name>: localhost/<profile-name>
```

